# {{ name }}: Agent Entry Point (a Corex client site)

This is a **Corex client site**, not the Corex framework. A copy of the framework sits beside the
site in this repository and is updated by merging each framework release, so
**do not edit Corex framework folders here.**

## Framework updates: the rule that keeps them conflict-free
Everything outside `sites/` and `.github/workflows/site-*.yml` is **framework-owned**, the
repository root included. Client work never edits a framework-owned path: an edit there is a merge
conflict waiting in every later framework update.

- `npm run verify:framework` proves the framework's files in this repository still match the
  release recorded in `corex-baseline.json`. Run it before pushing; the generated
  `site-*.yml` workflow runs it on every pull request.
- To take a framework release, follow `UPDATING-COREX.md`.
- If a framework defect blocks this site and cannot wait for a release, patch it and record the
  file under `exceptions` in `corex-baseline.json` with its reason and the upstream issue.

## Role Gate: you are in CLIENT SITE MODE
> Role Gate decides **where** you work · the spec decides **what** · Guard Gate decides **whether it ships** ·
> UI/UX ProMax decides **whether visible UI is good enough**.

- Edit **only** this client source (this plugin + theme + this site's `docs/`/`specs/`).
- **Do not** edit Corex framework internals (`plugins/`, `addons/`, `packages/`, the Corex parent `theme/`, root
  `specs/`, `ROADMAP.md`, root `PROGRESS.md`).
- **Do not** edit `wp/wp-content/` or `dist/` as source (runtime/build output; `dist/` is generated, never committed).
- Keep this site's specs in `specs/`, progress in `PROGRESS.md`, decisions in `DECISIONS.md`.
- Follow **the CoreX loop**, the **Guard Gate** and **UI/UX ProMax** for all UI work (this site's version of
  the loop is below).
- For a **framework** bug or a reusable block/component, **stop** and open a CoreX Framework Mode task in the
  framework repo: never patch CoreX internals for one client.

End every working response with the team's standard handoff format:
SUMMARY / WORKSPACE / MODE / SPEC STATUS / VERIFICATION / BLOCKERS / RECOMMENDED NEXT STEP, then the
`--- NEXT STEP ---` block.

## Where code goes
- **App / business code** → the site plugin `{{ plugin_slug }}/` (namespace `{{ namespace }}\`).
- **Presentation / theme code** → the site theme `{{ theme_slug }}/`.
- Site docs/specs → `docs/` and `specs/`.

The client uses its own identity, distinct from Corex: namespace `{{ namespace }}\`, text domain
`{{ text_domain }}`, REST namespace `{{ rest_namespace }}`, CSS prefix `{{ css_prefix }}`, option/CPT
prefix `{{ option_prefix }}`. Import Corex base classes; never use the `Corex\` namespace for app classes.

## Workflow (every non-trivial change)
1. Pull `develop`. 2. Branch `feature/<work-item>-<slug>`. 3. Write or update the spec in `specs/NNN-name/`.
4. Implement **only** in the client plugin/theme. 5. Run the guards/checks. 6. Update docs.
7. Commit. 8. Open a PR. 9. CI + review. 10. Merge to `develop` (staging) → tag/`main` (production).

**One feature = one branch = one spec folder = one PR.** Run the guard skills before presenting/pushing.
**Never push directly to `develop`/`main`.** Update docs after any feature.

**Generators.** `wp corex make:*` writes into `{{ plugin_slug }}/src` under `{{ namespace }}\` while this
is the only site in the repository. With more than one site, or to send them elsewhere, set `APP_PATH`,
`APP_NAMESPACE` and `APP_PREFIX` in the repository's `.env`.

**Specs.** This site's specs live in `sites/{{ site_dir }}/specs/NNN-name/spec.md`, numbered from 001 in this
site's own folder and never in the root `specs/`, which is the framework's: a spec there shows as drift in
`npm run verify:framework`.

## The CoreX loop, as this site runs it
The root `AGENTS.md`, "The CoreX loop", holds each step in full. For this site:

**Every file the loop writes is this site's own.** The root file names paths at the repository root, which
are the framework's: a file written there is drift in `npm run verify:framework`. Here they are
`sites/{{ site_dir }}/specs/`, `sites/{{ site_dir }}/DECISIONS.md`, `sites/{{ site_dir }}/PROGRESS.md`,
`sites/{{ site_dir }}/docs/glossary.md` (for the root's `docs/_glossary.md`) and
`sites/{{ site_dir }}/design/handoffs/` (for the root's `design/handoffs/`).

1. **Grill** one question at a time until nothing about the change is open. Decisions go into
   this site's `DECISIONS.md`.
2. **Research** only when the spec depends on outside facts: the findings go to `specs/NNN-name/research.md`.
3. **Spec** in `specs/NNN-name/spec.md`: requirements with ids (`FR-001`), ending in slices. Each slice is one
   pull request and names the slices it waits on.
4. **Owner-only steps** as a checked script in this site's folder, which walks the owner through each step and
   checks it.
5. **One slice at a time, test first.** Bugs start by reproducing the bug and proving its cause, then the
   regression test. A slice a person sees also follows "UI, UX and front end" below.
6. **Guard Gate**, then the measured UI review for a slice a person sees, then the two-model review
   (`debate-review --local`): one model finds problems, a model from another vendor tries to disprove them.
7. **Pull request**, its review rounds, and a retro after a release.

### UI, UX and front end
A slice a person sees runs these steps too, and one that skips a step is not done. The root `AGENTS.md`,
"UI, UX and front end", holds them in full.

1. **The client's approved design is the functional contract.** Build and measure from it, never from memory;
   nothing in it ships as a sample or a placeholder.
2. **What the design does not draw:** a screen or state it leaves out, for example an empty, loading or error
   state, is designed first and approved by the owner before it is built (in Claude Design, on the client's
   design).
3. **Build** with UI/UX ProMax (`ui-ux-pro-max`): tokens from the theme's `theme.json` only,
   logical CSS properties, assets loaded only where the block is, every string translatable in `{{ text_domain }}`.
4. **Measure the rendered page**, never judge it from a screenshot: gaps, heights and alignment read off the
   page and kept as browser assertions; 375px and 200% zoom; keyboard and focus; right to left.
5. **Review it** before the two-model review: `corex-ui-reviewer` compares the render with the approved
   design, an accessibility review checks WCAG 2.2 AA, and a copy review checks the words.
6. **One recorded round for the owner** at the end of the slice, never while building, on a scratch site with
   test data.

## Models, effort and agents (Claude Code)
| Work | Who | Model | Effort |
|---|---|---|---|
| Grilling, specs, decisions, final judgment on a diff | the main session | Fable | high |
| Building a slice, fixing a diagnosed bug | `corex-builder` | Opus | medium (high for a UI slice or a bug) |
| Tests, fixtures, translations, docs chores, the recorded review round | `corex-tester` | Sonnet | medium |
| Searching CoreX or this site | `corex-scout` | Haiku | low |
| Reviewing a diff against its spec and the constitution | `corex-reviewer` | Fable | high |
| A rendered page measured against its approved design | `corex-ui-reviewer` | Fable | high |
| The second review, from another vendor | the `debate-review` lane | Codex | high |

The agents are in the repository's `.claude/agents/`: framework-owned, carried with every release, so this
site does not edit them. Every brief to an agent says the work is in Client Site Mode and names this site's
folder (`sites/{{ site_dir }}/`) and the spec in it: an agent sees only its brief, and without that it takes
the work for the framework's and looks for the spec at the repository root. Max effort only when the owner asks, for one task. Agents never commit: the main
session reads every diff, runs the guards and commits.
